# Which Gene Is in the Middle?

@left

| Pair | Distance (cM) |
| --- | --- |
| [*AB*]{color=#D40000} | [*10*]{color=#D40000} |
| [*AC*]{color=#003FFF} | [*15*]{color=#003FFF} |
| [*BC*]{color=#A719DB} | [*24*]{color=#A719DB} |

@right

[*Step 6: Choose the Middle Gene*]{color=#A719DB}

- There are three choices: A, B, or C.
- Which pair is farthest apart?
- Those two genes are the endpoints.
- Place the remaining gene between them.

=== layout: stacked-panels

# A Is the Middle Gene

@top

![A is between B and C, with 10 and 15 cM adjacent intervals.](assets/lect09c-tetrad_worked_examples_bpw/gene_map.svg)

@bottom

- B-C is the largest pairwise estimate, so B and C are the endpoints.
- B-A-C and C-A-B describe the same order.
- The adjacent intervals come from A-B and A-C.

=== layout: two-panels

# Keep Pairwise Estimates Separate

@left

| Pair | Distance (cM) |
| --- | --- |
| [*AB*]{color=#D40000} | [*10*]{color=#D40000} |
| [*AC*]{color=#003FFF} | [*15*]{color=#003FFF} |
| [*BC*]{color=#A719DB} | [*24*]{color=#A719DB} |

@right

- Adjacent interval sum: 10 + 15 = *25 cM*.
- Direct B-C tetrad estimate: *24 cM*.
- These are estimates from different pairwise counts.
- Draw the map using its adjacent intervals; retain 24 cM as the calculated B-C answer.

@notes

Connect this small discrepancy to the distinction between pairwise estimates and interval sums from the testcross lecture. The tetrad correction assumes a crossover model; separate pairwise estimates need not add exactly. Do not change the source counts to force equality.

